---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro"
import SectionContainer from "@/components/SectionContainer.astro"
import CaseHeader from "@/components/CaseHeader.astro"

const WEEKS = [
  { semana: "W1", ventas: 40, costos: 28 },
  { semana: "W2", ventas: 46, costos: 30 },
  { semana: "W3", ventas: 44, costos: 36 },
  { semana: "W4", ventas: 58, costos: 34 },
  { semana: "W5", ventas: 52, costos: 33 },
  { semana: "W6", ventas: 49, costos: 38 },
  { semana: "W7", ventas: 63, costos: 37 },
  { semana: "W8", ventas: 61, costos: 36 },
]
const maxVentas = Math.max(...WEEKS.map((week) => week.ventas))
---

<Layout
  title="Sample sales reading | Diego Sarmiento"
  description="Practice data-analyst case with made-up figures: what to look at in a shop's sales and what would change."
>
  <main class="px-4 py-24">
    <SectionContainer>
      <CaseHeader
        kicker="Data analyst · practice case"
        title="What to look at when sales rise and profit does not"
        summary="Example figures, built to show the method. They are not from a client or a workshop. The question is the usual one: if sales grow, why does profit not follow?"
      />

      <section class="mt-14 space-y-4 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white">The problem</h2>
        <p>
          A neighborhood store sees the weekly till grow, but at closing there
          is not more money left. Without separating sales, costs and what has
          not been collected yet, the owner only sees a total and does not
          know where to adjust.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white">Sales and costs, in the example</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 mb-6 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          A 0 to 100 scale, only to compare weeks. The light bar is sales.
          The dark bar is cost.
        </p>
        <div class="space-y-3">
          {
            WEEKS.map((week) => (
              <div>
                <div class="mb-1 flex justify-between text-xs text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">
                  <span>{week.semana}</span>
                  <span>{week.ventas} / {week.costos}</span>
                </div>
                <div class="flex h-3 overflow-hidden rounded-sm bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800">
                  <div
                    class="h-full bg-yellow-500 dark:bg-yellow-300"
                    style={`width:${Math.round((week.ventas / maxVentas) * 100)}%`}
                  />
                </div>
                <div class="mt-1 flex h-2 overflow-hidden rounded-sm bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800">
                  <div
                    class="h-full bg-gray-400 dark:bg-gray-500"
                    style={`width:${Math.round((week.costos / maxVentas) * 100)}%`}
                  />
                </div>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-12 space-y-4 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white">What shows up</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
          <li>Sales rise toward the end, but in W3 and W6 cost gets too close to sales.</li>
          <li>Those two weeks are where to ask: was too much bought, or was it sold too cheap?</li>
          <li>The full month hides those two dips. That is why the week matters more than the monthly close.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-12 space-y-4 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white">What would improve</h2>
        <ol class="list-decimal space-y-3 pl-5">
          <li>Each week, separate what was sold, what was spent and what has not reached the till yet.</li>
          <li>Review W3 and W6 purchases before repeating the same “just in case” order.</li>
          <li>Do not decide the month from one number. The total hides the weak weeks.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">
          This is an exercise to show how I read a business. On a real job I
          use the client's numbers in private, and I do not publish them.
        </p>
      </section>
    </SectionContainer>
  </main>
</Layout>
